    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so it's probably nitrogen. i tried adding some compost but the yellowing is still happening. is there anything else that could be \"stealing\" the nutrients away from the plant? like could something be hiding in the soil?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, something could be \"stealing\" nutrients—root-knot nematodes are microscopic worms that attack tomato roots, creating galls that block water and nutrient uptake, even if you add fertilizer. Also, competing weeds or nearby plants with aggressive roots (like grass) can siphon nitrogen before your tomato gets it. Check the roots by gently digging around the base; if they look swollen or knotted, remove the plant and solarize the soil. For now, try a quick-release liquid fish emulsion to bypass the root damage, and keep the soil consistently moist but not soggy to reduce stress.",
